4. Compare Nessus and OpenVAS by listing 3 features or differences between them that would matter if you were scanning a real-world app like Flipkart or Myntra.
=>When assessing a large e-commerce platform (complex infrastructure, many assets, compliance needs, high accuracy requirements):
Licensing / Scale limits
Nessus Essentials is free but capped at a small number of IP addresses (commonly cited as 5–16 depending on current Tenable policy). OpenVAS (Greenbone Community Edition) is free with no host/IP limit. For anything beyond a tiny lab, this quickly forces a paid Nessus license or a switch to OpenVAS.
Ease of use, reporting quality, and false-positive rate
Nessus generally offers a more polished UI, faster setup, cleaner professional reports, and lower false-positive rates. OpenVAS is fully functional but typically requires more tuning, has a steeper initial setup, and produces more noise that needs manual review—important when producing client-ready findings for a large retail target.
Plugin coverage, update speed, and compliance features
Both have strong vulnerability coverage, but Nessus is often preferred for rapid daily plugin updates, accuracy claims, and richer built-in compliance templates (CIS, PCI-DSS, etc.) in paid versions. OpenVAS provides good open-source coverage and full self-hosting control, but commercial/compliance features and support are stronger or more convenient on the Tenable side for enterprise-style assessments.
